  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Sunday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Skies are mostly clear at this time\nacross northern and central Nevada. For later today, an upper\ntrough that is across central and southern California will drift\nto the east and northeast across the forecast area. Subtropical \nmoisture from the south will be pulled northward towards central \nNevada. Precipitable water values are forecast to increase to 0.60\nto 0.80 inches by later this afternoon. Look for isolated to \nscattered showers and thunderstorms developing later this morning \nand afternoon. Main threats today will be wind gusts to 55 mph, \nsmall hail, dangerous cloud to ground lightning, Locally brief, \nmoderate to heavy rain is also possible with any storm. Highs \ntoday will be in the 80s for the most part, though in central \nNevada where cloud cover will be most prevalent, highs will be in \nthe 70s. Look for the activity to diminish by the early evening \nwith lows in the 40s and 50s.\n\nOn Tuesday, some remnant subtropical moisture will exist over the\nnortheast parts of Elko county, resulting in isolated to scattered\nshowers and thunderstorms in the afternoon. otherwise, most areas\nwill see mostly sunny skies. Winds will be breezy across the area\nwith gusts to 30 mph in spots. Highs will be in the 70s and 80s\nwith overnight lows in the 40s.\n\nFrom Wednesday through Friday, an upper level trough in the\nPacific Northwest will wobble with bits of energy rounding the\nbase of the trough and moving through the western United States.\nAt this time, moisture appears scant with breezy afternoon winds\nto 20 to 30 mph. Highs will be in the 70s and 80s with overnight \nlows in the 40s.\n\nSaturday and Sunday, there is a tropical system across the eastern\nPacific at this time that is expected to move north towards Baja\nCalifornia. The aforementioned upper trough in the Pacific\nNorthwest will shift slowly to the south. By the weekend, there\nare indications these two systems may interact with each other,\nallowing for moisture to be drawn northward. At this time, very\nlow confidence exists in this scenario and it will need to be\nwatched closely. PoPs and associated gridded forecast will\ncontinue with scattered showers and thunderstorms for the upcoming\nweekend but timing, coverage, and extent all are variables that\nwill need to be further refined in the coming days. Highs will be\nin the 70s and 80s with lows in the 40s.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: A few adjustments were made to\nthe PoPs, Wx, and winds to reflect current and ongoing \nconditions. Confidence is moderate to high for precipitation \nchances this afternoon, but remains low with respect to amounts \nand location of convective activity. Confidence is moderate at \nthis time regarding the mid-week break in weather. Confidence is \nvery low for this weekend's possible weekend storm.",
